;
; LIN 2.x commander (LIN 1.x: master) for the MVP chip.
;
; One SM cycle per bit time.  Every bit instruction has delay 0, so a bit lasts exactly one
; CLKDIV period and CLKDIV is the only thing that sets the bit rate: fw/lin/driver.py builds
; the divisor from the words below (`sm_cycles_per_bit`, read out of the assembled program)
; and hands the host the word stream to push.
;
; The wire
; --------
; LIN is one wire, open drain: 0 is dominant (driven), 1 is recessive (released, the bus
; pull-up holds it up).  The MVP has no transceiver and no OE inversion
; (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Open drain`, `## Differences from RP2040` item 10), so the program
; emulates open drain the way docs/mvp-isa.md prescribes: the pin's *level* register stays at
; its reset value 0 and the program drives the *direction*.  `side 1` pulls the line low,
; `side 2` releases it, and the data bits come out of the OSR through `out pindirs, 1`.
;
; The LIN pin must therefore be one of the bidirectional uio pins, 0..7: PINDIRS cannot drive
; the input-only (8..12) or output-only (13..17) protocol pins (`### The 18 protocol pins`).
;
; The side-set channel
; --------------------
; `.side_set 1 opt pindirs` is the whole trick, and it is worth reading twice:
;
;   * SIDESET_COUNT = 2 -- one side-set *data* bit plus the `opt` enable bit -- with
;     EXECCTRL.SIDE_EN = 1, so the top bit of the side-set field is an enable and the low one
;     is data.  The assembler writes that enable itself for any non-zero operand
;     (asm/pioasm.py:468), so `side 1` means "enable, data 1" and `side 2` means
;     "enable, data 0": a side-set with a zero data bit.  An instruction with no `side`
;     operand has an all-zero field, the enable is 0, and **no side-set happens at all** --
;     which is what lets `out pindirs, 1` own the pin for a data bit.  Side-set wins over the
;     OUT/SET channel within one cycle (`docs/mvp-isa.md` `### Pin writes`), so a side-set on
;     a data-bit instruction would overwrite the very bit the OSR just asked for.
;   * SIDE_PINDIR = 1, so the side-set writes pin *directions*: 1 = drive (the line goes to
;     the level register, which is 0), 0 = release.  A LIN wire is never driven high, which
;     is also what lets a future responder read the same pin back (a node that cannot release
;     the wire can never listen to it).
;   * SIDESET_COUNT = 2 also leaves the delay field three bits wide, so an instruction lasts
;     1..8 SM cycles.  The 13-bit-time break field therefore costs two instructions
;     (8 + 6 = 14 bit times) rather than one; that is the price of the `opt` bit and it is
;     why the break is not a single `nop ... [15]`.
;
; SHIFTCTRL: AUTOPULL = 1, PULL_THRESH = 8, OUT_SHIFTDIR = 1 (its reset value, shift right).
; A LIN byte field is a start bit, eight data bits **least significant first** and a stop bit,
; so the eight `out pindirs, 1` of a field shift one OSR bit each and the eighth brings the
; shift count to the threshold, which refills the OSR from the TX FIFO in the same SM cycle
; (`docs/mvp-isa.md` `### Instruction semantics`, OUT).  The host pushes every byte
; right-justified with its bits already inverted for the direction register
; (`fw/lin/driver.py` `wire_word`), because 1 = drive = dominant = a zero data bit.
;
; PINCTRL: SIDESET_BASE = OUT_BASE = SET_BASE = the LIN pin, SIDESET_COUNT = 2, OUT_COUNT = 1
; and SET_COUNT = 1, so all three channels cover that one pin and nothing else.
; IN_BASE is unused (a commander never reads the wire) but points at the pin as well.
;
; What the host does
; ------------------
; Per frame, all through `SMe_INSTR`, which is the only way to set the PC
; (`docs/mvp-isa.md` `### Enable, restart`):
;
;   set x, 1              two byte fields in the header (sync 0x55, protected identifier)
;   set y, N              response byte fields still to send: 0 = header only (a responder
;                         on the bus answers), N > 0 = the chip publishes N fields itself
;                         (N data bytes and the checksum), see `jmp y-- resp`
;   jmp frame             start
;   TX FIFO: ~0x55, ~PID [, ~data..., ~checksum]
;
; Both counters are decremented by the loop's stop-bit instruction, which doubles as the stop
; bit and as the branch, so a byte field is exactly ten SM cycles -- ten bit times, the
; nominal T_Frame_Nominal -- with no inter-byte space at all.
;
; The pin
; -------
; No `wait`, no `in`, no IRQ.  A commander with nothing to say leaves the wire recessive in
; `idle` forever, and the host sees where it is in `SMe_ADDR`; there is no `irq wait` to park
; in (docs/mvp-isa.md `## Differences from RP2040` item 3).
;

.program lin
.side_set 1 opt pindirs

.wrap_target idle
.wrap idle

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; frame: the break field, the break delimiter, then the header's two byte fields.
; Entered by the host's forced `jmp frame`.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
frame:
        nop side 1 [7]                 ; break, part 1: 8 bit times dominant
        nop side 1 [5]                 ; break, part 2: 6 more, so 14 in all --
                                       ;   the spec floor is 13 (tBREAK) and one bit time of
                                       ;   margin covers the rounding of the bit period
        nop side 2 [1]                 ; break delimiter: 2 bit times recessive (>= 1,
                                       ;   tDELIM), which is also the first 2 bit times of
                                       ;   the space in front of the sync field

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One header byte field: start bit, 8 data bits LSB first, stop bit.  10 SM cycles.
; X counts the fields still to send (2 for the header), and the stop-bit instruction is the
; `jmp x--` that ends the field, so the counter costs no bit time.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
hdr:
        pull block side 1              ; start bit (side-set: drive, dominant) and the next
                                       ;   OSR word in the same SM cycle.  A `pull` here is a
                                       ;   barrier while the OSR is empty (autopull keeps
                                       ;   osr_cnt at 0 after a refill) and a real pop
                                       ;   otherwise, which is what primes the first field
                                       ;   after a restart and refills between fields.
        out pindirs, 1                 ; data bit 0 ... bit 7, one per SM cycle
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        jmp x-- hdr side 2             ; stop bit (recessive); X-- and loop while X != 0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The header is over.  Y is the number of response byte fields the host said it would
; publish: 0 falls through to `idle` and leaves the wire to a responder, anything else
; publishes.  The two SM cycles here are the response space, and the delay makes them two
; bit times, which is the response space a real commander leaves (a cluster that agrees on
; more is satisfied too; tRS's own floor is zero).
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        jmp y-- resp side 2 [1]

idle:
        jmp idle side 2               ; recessive, forever.  Also the wrap target.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One response byte field: identical to the header's, 10 SM cycles.  Y counts the fields,
; so the host sets Y to (data bytes + the checksum) before the frame starts.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
resp:
        pull block side 1              ; start bit + OSR refill
        out pindirs, 1                 ; data bit 0 ... bit 7
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        out pindirs, 1
        jmp y-- resp side 2            ; stop bit; Y-- and loop while Y != 0
        jmp idle side 2                ; the response is over: back to the idle wire